The issue arises from passing a raw pointer to `deleteSubtree`, causing ownership ambiguity issues with `std::unique_ptr`. To correct this, you should consider changing the signature of `deleteSubtree` to accept a `std::unique_ptr` directly, but since this might affect other parts of your code, a more straightforward change could be to adjust `Container::clear`. 

However, if you want to minimize changes and focus solely on the `deleteSubtree` issue, you can modify `Container::clear` to pass the unique_ptr instead:

```cpp
void Container::clear() {
    for (auto& node: nodes) {
        deleteSubtree(node); // Pass the unique_ptr instead
    }
    nodes.clear();
}

